        <body>
            <div xml:id="d759">

                <pb facs="0782" n="778" sameAs="frus:frus1939v03/pg778"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.811/1092: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Canton</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Myers</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Canton</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-02-05T16:00:00+08:00">February 5, 1939—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received February 5—2 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>21. The Pearl River has been closed as from February 3 on account of military
                    operations. This action which prevents movement of gunboats between Canton and
                    Hong Kong may it is surmised indicate the withdrawal of some local Japanese
                    units from this area for use in operations against Kwangsi Province or the
                    initiation of minor operations in the delta. The recent Japanese occupation of
                    Weichow Island in Gulf of Tongking would seem to lend weight to first
                    possibility.</p>
                <p>Indications are that the river will be opened on or about February 10.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Chungking, Peiping, Hong Kong.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Myers</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
